WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.586 --> 00:00:09.349
When China and Britain clashed in the first and second Opium Wars, it was over much more than

2
00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:12.191
the illegal drug for which these conflicts are named.

3
00:00:12.951 --> 00:00:20.035
The two nineteenth—century disputes shaped the history of modern China, leading to the downfall of its last ruling

4
00:00:20.095 --> 00:00:25.938
dynasty and exposing the nation to Western influences and economic domination.

5
00:00:26.779 --> 00:00:32.534
A century and a half later, China was still dealing with the consequences of the wars when the

6
00:00:32.594 --> 00:00:35.895
city of Hong Kong was at last returned to its control.

7
00:00:36.996 --> 00:00:41.658
In the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries, China was the greatest power in Asia.

8
00:00:42.438 --> 00:00:46.600
The emperors of the Qing dynasty had ruled since 1644.

9
00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.303
This was also a time of great expansion in trade and conquest by European nations.

10
00:00:53.163 --> 00:01:00.661
The Spanish controlled the Philippines; the Portuguese were established in Macau, an island on China's southern coast; and

11
00:01:00.701 --> 00:01:04.382
the British had conquered China's oldest and largest neighbor, India.

12
00:01:04.983 --> 00:01:06.723
But China was closed to Europe.

13
00:01:07.423 --> 00:01:13.605
China was a vast nation with a self—sufficient economy, and the Qing government saw little reason to open

14
00:01:13.665 --> 00:01:16.246
its doors to the West.

15
00:01:16.386 --> 00:01:23.207
In 1793, the emperor told an ambassador from Britain seeking increased trade that China had no need for

16
00:01:23.267 --> 00:01:24.908
Britain’s manufactured goods.

17
00:01:25.628 --> 00:01:33.135
International trade was allowed only at the port of Guangzhou, then called Canton, where foreign merchants were restricted

18
00:01:33.175 --> 00:01:35.257
to a small area outside the city.

19
00:01:36.078 --> 00:01:41.183
They were subject to Chinese law and could trade only with designated Chinese merchants.

20
00:01:42.504 --> 00:01:48.910
These restrictive trade policies were a deliberate effort to limit Chinese contact with foreigners and to protect the

21
00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:50.732
country from Western influence.

22
00:01:51.828 --> 00:01:58.792
The Canton system, as China's trading policy was known in the 1700s, was strictly enforced, and it led

23
00:01:58.852 --> 00:02:01.153
to a trade imbalance in China's favor.

24
00:02:01.814 --> 00:02:09.018
China was selling huge quantities of porcelain, silk, and especially tea to European nations, but was buying little

25
00:02:09.098 --> 00:02:09.698
in return.

26
00:02:10.579 --> 00:02:17.143
Britain imported millions of kilograms of tea from China every year, but 90 percent of the material brought

27
00:02:17.183 --> 00:02:19.284
to China on British ships was silver.

28
00:02:19.924 --> 00:02:23.007
The trade imbalance was depleting Britain's silver reserves.

29
00:02:23.847 --> 00:02:29.112
In a calculated effort to reverse this trend, Britain had begun exporting opium to China.

30
00:02:29.872 --> 00:02:33.795
Opium is a highly addictive drug made from the seeds of the poppy plant.

31
00:02:34.436 --> 00:02:39.920
The Chinese grew a very small amount of opium, and its use was limited mainly to the upper

32
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:47.774
classes. Nonetheless, it was recognized as a harmful substance, and Chinese emperors had passed laws against its use

33
00:02:48.175 --> 00:02:50.016
as early as 1729.

34
00:02:50.776 --> 00:02:56.340
Britain had a ready supply of the drug in its Indian colonies, where poppies were grown and harvested.

35
00:02:57.041 --> 00:03:04.346
In 1773, the British East India Company began a policy of selling opium to British traders, and by

36
00:03:04.386 --> 00:03:10.570
the end of the eighteenth century, Britain had increased exports of opium from two hundred chests a year

37
00:03:10.871 --> 00:03:14.453
to nearly four thousand.

38
00:03:14.533 --> 00:03:22.077
From 1796 to 1799, and again in 1810, the Chinese government extended its laws against the use, production,

39
00:03:22.237 --> 00:03:23.378
and sale of opium.

40
00:03:24.078 --> 00:03:28.861
The British ignored these laws and continued to bring the drug to the shores of Guangzhou.

41
00:03:29.502 --> 00:03:33.364
By 1830, opium addiction was a major problem in China.

42
00:03:34.184 --> 00:03:40.148
Most men under the age of forty smoked opium regularly, including many members of the Chinese army.

43
00:03:40.908 --> 00:03:47.111
Britain's trade deficit with China had reversed to become a trade surplus, and silver was now flowing from

44
00:03:47.191 --> 00:03:48.231
China to England.

45
00:03:49.012 --> 00:03:54.714
The Chinese government was extremely concerned about both the addiction and the corruption that the opium trade was

46
00:03:54.755 --> 00:03:56.195
causing among its officials.

47
00:03:57.116 --> 00:04:04.779
In March of 1839, the emperor sent Lin Zexu, an official renowned for his integrity, to Guangzhou to

48
00:04:04.839 --> 00:04:06.260
enforce the ban on opium.

49
00:04:07.464 --> 00:04:13.646
In two months, he made 1,600 arrests and confiscated 11,000 pounds of opium.

50
00:04:14.486 --> 00:04:20.548
When Lin seized another twenty thousand chests of opium, destroyed them, and threw them into the sea, the

51
00:04:20.588 --> 00:04:27.690
British saw this as unlawful destruction of their property and opened hostilities, beginning the first Opium War.

52
00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:34.132
The British navy was the greatest military power in the world, and China's navy was quickly defeated.

53
00:04:34.836 --> 00:04:41.480
The Treaty of Nanjing ended the war in 1842, and its terms were so unfavorable to China that

54
00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:45.423
historians consider it the first of what they call the "Unequal Treaties."

55
00:04:46.243 --> 00:04:53.928
The treaty forced China to open four more ports to foreign trade (Fuzhou, Xiamen, Ningbo, and Shanghai), to

56
00:04:53.968 --> 00:04:59.132
pay a fine of $21 million in silver, and to give the island of Hong Kong to the

57
00:04:59.172 --> 00:05:05.252
British. The restrictive Canton system was replaced by new trading rules that favored the British.

58
00:05:06.453 --> 00:05:08.815
Trading in opium, however, was still illegal.

59
00:05:09.495 --> 00:05:12.658
Nevertheless, Britain continued to export the drug to China.

60
00:05:13.338 --> 00:05:19.183
By the outbreak of the second Opium War, shipments of opium to China had reached a staggering seventy

61
00:05:19.243 --> 00:05:20.624
thousand chests per year.

62
00:05:21.605 --> 00:05:27.969
Under the new trade laws, British nationals were exempt from Chinese law, so shipowners wanting to evade the

63
00:05:28.009 --> 00:05:30.750
law registered their boats with the British.

64
00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:37.913
In 1856, Chinese officials arrested the crew of the Arrow, a boat suspected of piracy and smuggling but

65
00:05:37.993 --> 00:05:40.474
registered with British authorities in Hong Kong.

66
00:05:41.195 --> 00:05:47.598
Britain took advantage of the situation to accuse the Chinese of meddling in British affairs and attacked Guangzhou.

67
00:05:48.278 --> 00:05:54.257
France joined Britain in this second Opium War, also known as the Arrow War, and two years later,

68
00:05:54.397 --> 00:05:56.199
a new Unequal Treaty was signed.

69
00:05:57.040 --> 00:06:03.605
The Treaty of Tianjin required China to pay new fines, open up new ports, legalize the opium trade,

70
00:06:03.906 --> 00:06:06.128
and give Britain further rights in China.

71
00:06:06.948 --> 00:06:15.075
Disputes over the treaty continued until 1860, finally ending when Franco—British forces marched on Beijing and set fire

72
00:06:15.115 --> 00:06:16.737
to the emperor’s Summer Palace.

73
00:06:17.457 --> 00:06:20.020
The Chinese had been defeated and humiliated.

74
00:06:20.724 --> 00:06:23.707
The effects of the Opium Wars on China were far—reaching.

75
00:06:24.427 --> 00:06:30.352
The Unequal Treaties weakened the power of the Qing government and led to internal rebellions and the dynasty’s

76
00:06:30.392 --> 00:06:31.393
eventual demise.

77
00:06:32.154 --> 00:06:39.820
Britain, France, Russia, Germany, and Japan were all granted control over vast Chinese erritories in an arrangement called

78
00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:41.181
“spheres of influence.”

79
00:06:42.082 --> 00:06:47.767
Hong Kong and the Kowloon Peninsula were ceded to Britain, and Russia gained a large amount of territory

80
00:06:47.867 --> 00:06:48.808
in northern China.

81
00:06:49.544 --> 00:06:54.349
The country’s once strong and self—sufficient economy was brought to the point of collapse.

82
00:06:55.370 --> 00:07:00.615
The Opium Wars, however, were not just about opium and silver, but about power and the race to

83
00:07:00.655 --> 00:07:01.356
rule the world.

84
00:07:02.117 --> 00:07:08.363
China represented the last prize in the Far East for European countries, and these wars were carefully designed

85
00:07:08.403 --> 00:07:12.788
steps meant to "open" China—against the country's will, if necessary.

86
00:07:13.524 --> 00:07:20.348
The long—lasting humiliation caused by the Unequal Treaties gave rise to a strong nationalism among Chinese people.

87
00:07:21.069 --> 00:07:27.993
This nationalism, resentment of the West, and the hardships people suffered adapting to an industrial economy helped bring

88
00:07:28.053 --> 00:07:29.834
China’s communist party to power.

89
00:07:30.795 --> 00:07:37.139
In 1949, the communist leader Mao Zedong declared the formation of the People’s Republic of China, as the

90
00:07:37.179 --> 00:07:38.240
country is known today.
